  "worked_solution": "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{\u2032}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =i+t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $r(t)$ and ${r}^{\u2032}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $r(t)\\cdot {r}^{\u2032}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)\\cdot {r}^{\u2032}(t) & =0 \\\\ [t\\,i+(1+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2})\\,j]\\cdot (i+t\\,j) & =0 \\\\ t\\cdot 1+(1+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2})\\cdot t & =0 \\\\ 2t+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{3} & =0 \\\\ t(4+{t}^{2}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $t=0$ since $4+{t}^{2}>0$ for any $t.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0)=0\\,i+(1+\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2})\\,j=j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n we conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{\u2032}(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(0,1).$",
